WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.880
Baby formula is powdered milk, oil, and sugar at a pediatric premium. The atoms are cheap, the can is not. This is First Principles Daily, Episode 80. Walk a United States grocery aisle and pick up a name brand can of powdered infant formula. A typical tub holds somewhere around a pound and a half of beige powder, and that tub commonly sits in the mid-twenties to the high thirties of dollars. Specialty cans climb from there. Infant formula is one of the most tightly specified

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:40.520
foods on earth, and the tightness is not a fashion. A newborn may take in nothing else for months, so the recipe is treated as a medical specification rather than a grocery mix.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:49.520
Federal rules set floors and ceilings for protein, fat, linoleic acid, and a long list of vitamins and minerals in every hundred calories.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:01:05.160
A plant that wants to sell a new powder must register, follow infant formula manufacturing practice, test both nutrients and the dry powder pathogens that have historically caused the scares, and weighed out a notification process before the first can ships.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:17.580
After earlier failures of composition and quality, that legal structure was built so this food could not quietly drift. Inside the industry the resulting line feels like the only adult way to do the job.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:27.380
Wet blend the oils into a dairy stream, homogenize, heat treat, spray dry, nitrogen flush a can, hold the lot for release testing.

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:37.000
The commercial structure on top of those rules is what makes the shelf price feel normal. A handful of manufacturers have long occupied most of the American aisle.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:50.280
Pediatricians recommend the labels they saw in training. Hospitals send families home with a particular can in the discharge bag, and that first can is a switching cost no economist has to explain to a sleep deprived parent.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:57.920
A large public nutrition program buys formula for a large share of United States infants under sole source state contracts.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:05.740
One brand wins, often by offering a deep rebate, and that brand becomes what clinics and end caps treat as a default.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:18.940
Marketing, medical detailing, and the liability posture of a product that cannot be allowed to fail, a treat on top of a spray dried powder that is at bottom, milk sugar, vegetable fat, and dairy protein.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:31.900
From inside the category, none of this looks like a puzzle. It looks like caution. Caution plus concentration plus a buyer who will not experiment is how a commodity dairy blend becomes a pediatric premium.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:40.520
Now read a kilogram of finished cow's milk powder, the way an engineer would, because that is the unit the commodities are actually sold in.

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:49.660
Something like half to a bit more than half the weight is carbohydrate, and in a standard formula that carbohydrate is almost entirely lactose.

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:59.900
A little over a quarter is fat, almost always a blend of vegetable oils, palm, coconut, soy, sunflower, or the high oleic cousins of those same oils.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:11.820
A little over a tenth is protein, as way and casing from cow's milk. Moisture, minerals, and a vitamin and lipid pre-mix make up the rest, and those leftovers weigh almost nothing.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:18.160
Price those atoms as commodities, and treat every figure as a working estimate rather than an invoice.

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:27.960
Lactose is a cheese industry byproduct, not a rare earth. Industrial edible lactose commonly moves at roughly a dollar to a couple of dollars a kilogram,

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:34.080
which puts the lactose inside one kilo of formula somewhere around 50 cents to about a dollar 50.

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:46.700
The oil blend is grocery store chemistry, commodity vegetable oils often trade at roughly a dollar to a few dollars a kilogram, so a quarter kilo of that fat is something like 30 cents to a dollar.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:52.780
Protein is the expensive macronutrient, and even here we are still in the dairy complex.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:57.580
Way protein concentrate and skim milk solids, not a fermenter drug.

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:11.100
Take a wide band, several dollars to a bit over ten dollars a kilogram of protein-rich dairy powder, depending on grade and the year, and the protein in one kilo of formula still lands near a dollar than a ten dollar bill.

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:22.800
The macronutrient premix and the specialty long chain lipids added for dachosa hexanoic acid, or DHA, and a racodonic acid, or ARA, punch above their weight.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:31.420
Those additives are a tiny mass fraction and a real purchase order line, which is why a magic wand number that ignored them would be cheating.

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:45.340
Even being generous, treating those premixes as costing as much again as the bulk lactose, oil and protein per together, the raw material floor for a kilogram of powder still sits in the low to mid single digit dollars.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:51.620
A rough magic wand estimate of about two to six dollars a kilo is honest enough to reason with.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:58.420
Wave the wand, arrange the raw atoms into finished powder for free, and that is the theoretical floor.

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:01.580
The price of the stuff if arranging it cost nothing.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:16.480
The shelf is a different number, a name brand can holding roughly six to 900 grams, and selling for something in the mid twenties to around $40 is asking in round numbers, something like $35 to $60 per kilogram.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:20.180
A small hypoallergenic can can run higher still.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:33.040
Divide that finished price by a two to $6 material floor, and the idiot index, finished cost over material cost, offered here as arithmetic you can redo, not as a measured factory metric.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:37.200
Land somewhere around 8 to 20 for ordinary name brand powder.

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:44.900
Extensively hydrolyzed and amino acid formulas stretch it, because those products have genuinely done more work to the protein.

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:51.840
The integer is not the point. The point is that you are many times the atoms, and not because the atoms are exotic.

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:58.740
Where does the multiple actually live? Walk the can backward. Spray drying is real industrial work.

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:04.340
A sound-centrated liquid is atomized into a tower of hot air so water flashes off in seconds.

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:09.340
And the dryer, the evaporators upstream of it, and the energy bill are not free.

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:18.840
They are also a mature, cheese plant technology, not a rocket engine, and they do not, by themselves, turn two dollars of solids into $40 of powder.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:25.420
Wet blending, homogenization, and heat treatment add capital and yield loss in the same bounded way.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:32.000
Pathogen control is a heavier line than outsiders expect, because powdered formula is not sterile.

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:39.920
Cronobacter, the organism that has driven the worst modern recalls, lives in dry environments and is a genuine infant hazard.

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:48.040
So plants run environmental monitoring, hold and release testing, and a quality system that looks more like a drug plant than a powdered milkshed.

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:53.000
That costs money. It does not cost a tenfold markup on lactose.

00:06:53.700 --> 00:07:00.240
Packaging is visible and over-explained. A steel or composite can, a scoop, a foil diaphragm,

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:05.000
and a nitrogen flush are a few dollars of converted metal and plastic, not the gap.

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:13.360
The specialty lipids and the vitamin ticket are a bigger share of the ingredient bill than their gram suggest, and they are also a branding handle.

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:20.060
Once DHA is on the front of the can, the product has stepped out of the dairy case and into the pediatric aisle.

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:26.280
Regulatory overhead and the time to qualify a new dryer or a new recipe are a carrying cost.

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:32.120
Capacity that takes years to stand up will not compete away a margin the way a new snack line will.

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:36.700
The rest of the multiple is not on the factory floor. It is in the channel.

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:44.260
Brand advertising and pediatric detailing teach a frightened buyer that these beige powders are not substitutes for one another.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:49.220
The discharge bag and the sole-source public contract lock that lesson in.

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:52.820
Wholesale and retail take their cut on a high list price.

00:07:53.420 --> 00:08:04.680
The rebate structure on those public contracts can actually push list prices up rather than down, because the fight is over the rebate, not over the unsubsidized shelf tag the non-program parent pays.

00:08:05.420 --> 00:08:08.000
Ready to feed liquid makes the same logic louder.

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:14.920
You are then paying to sterilize and to ship water, and the idiot index against the dissolved solids gets worse,

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:19.160
which is why a powder is already the cheap format and still not cheap.

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:28.360
Add those stages and you can see a path from a few dollars of commodities to a $30 can without once discovering a missing expensive atom.

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:33.280
The waste is in process, packaging, trust machinery, and lock-in.

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:40.200
And the lock-in is the thickest layer. A from scratch redesign would not start by inventing a new baby food.

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:43.840
It would start by telling the truth about the one we already have.

00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:51.200
This is a nutrient-specified dairy powder, and the lactose and whey in it often began as the leftover of cheese making.

00:08:51.780 --> 00:09:02.560
The first move is to co-locate the dryer with those streams, and to run formula as a high-care product on a dairy solid's campus, rather than as a standalone pharmaceutical object.

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:13.120
Store brand cans already prove that a lower price is manufacturable from the same United States Department of Agriculture grade inputs and the same federal nutrient table.

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:18.260
What has to be true for that move to cut toward the floor is boring and specific.

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:30.800
Inspected capacity next to a whey silo, a wet process that stays inside the pathogen spec, and a purchase contract for oils and premixes that does not treat every additive as a brand exclusive.

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:40.020
The second move is the can itself. Multi-serve pouches and simpler barrier packs already exist in other markets, and in some domestic lines.

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:45.620
They do not change the powder, and they do shave conversion cost, cube and shipping mass.

00:09:46.300 --> 00:09:53.120
What has to be true is oxygen and moisture barrier, good enough that the fat does not oxidize, and the powder does not cake.

00:09:53.120 --> 00:10:04.140
Plus a filling line that keeps the dry product dry. That is packaging engineering, not a new theory of infant nutrition, and it will not by itself collapse an idiot index of 10.

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:08.140
It is the easy second cut once the plant is in the right place.

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:11.820
The third move is the channel and the rules that freeze it.

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:26.040
Soul source public contracts, hospital discharge kits, and a notification and inspection timeline that makes a new dryer a multi-year bet are why a handful of labels can hold a pediatric premium on a cheese byproduct.

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:33.420
A redesign that wants the cost to fall has to bid the public program on landed cost rather than on rebate theater.

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:44.820
Put a generic can in the discharge bag, and make inspected capacity something you can add as if it were a dairy dryer with a special quality system, not a once-a-generation factory.

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:56.260
The prize is not a race to an unsafe floor. It is a store brand-like price on every standard can, with the remaining premium reserved for the products that have actually done more chemistry.

00:10:56.820 --> 00:11:06.300
The hard parts are real and should be named without romance. Heat that would reliably sterilize low moisture powder also damages the nutrients the statute requires.

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:11.300
So the control has to be the process and the building, not a terminal kill step.

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:17.400
Parents will pay for trust, and they should. A hungry infant is not a place to A-B test a brand.

00:11:18.260 --> 00:11:30.460
Extensively hydrolyzed and amino acid formulas for cow's milk allergy have a genuinely higher material and process bill, because enzymes, filtration, and free amino acids are not skim milk powder.

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:38.160
Liability follows any new plant for decades. None of that justifies a double digit idiot index on the ordinary can.

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:46.300
It justifies treating the ordinary can as dairy, and the specialty can as the place where the extra money actually bought extra work.

00:11:46.300 --> 00:11:58.980
When the customer cannot walk away, a hungry newborn, a discharge bag, a state contract, the gap between the can and the atoms stops being a manufacturing residual and becomes a measure of lock-in.

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:03.160
A nutrient table is a recipe constraint, not a price floor.

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:14.220
Hitting every milligram of iron and linoleic acid the government named does not require a luxury label, only a dryer, a pre-mix, and a lab that can prove the batch.

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:19.980
The next person who wants this category to move does not need a new theory of infant nutrition.

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:31.700
That person needs a dryer that already sits beside a way silo, a wet process that stays inside the pathogen spec, and a purchase book that treats oils and pre-mixes as commodities.

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:40.320
Carry that into the aisle. Who files the next infant formula notification for that dryer? And what is the first signal they are serious?

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:49.020
A public program bid at a landed cost the incumbents cannot match, or a store brand shelf tag that finally stops shadowing the name brands.

00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:55.320
That's a wrap on today's first principles breakdown. The ideas travel further when you pass them on.

